{:edb/db-before-t 165 :edb/db-after-t 166 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533478 50 #inst "2026-10-11T02:44:26.690Z" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186054611 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403271 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186054823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404141 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186054824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399370 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186054824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399377 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059691 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403150 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059691 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403157 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059693 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401728 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059693 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401735 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401742 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404210 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404217 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059698 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399530 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059698 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399551 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401701 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401708 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401715 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059742 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400756 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401694 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059772 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400232 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059772 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403264 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400386 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400393 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401839 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401157 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401164 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401171 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400351 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400358 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400365 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402615 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403341 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059855 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400372 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057000 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401613 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060112 1071 "$3 \\mid 18$ or $11$ is not prime.\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060112 1071 "$\\,$ $3\\,|\\,18$ or $11$ is not prime.\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060112 1074 "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that the negation of a disjunction of two statements is equivalent to the conjunction of the negations of the individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n¬(A∨B)=¬A∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(3\\,|\\,18\\text{ or }11\\text{ is not prime}) & ≡¬((3\\,|\\,18)∨(11\\text{ is not prime})) \\\\  & ≡¬(3\\,|\\,18)∧¬(11\\text{ is not prime}) \\\\  & ≡(3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,18)∧(11\\text{ is prime}) \\\\  & ≡3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,18\\text{ and }11\\text{ is prime}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057218 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401722 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060147 1071 "Given that the minor of $p_{23}$ is $t^{2} + 12$, find all the possible values of $t$ in the matrix\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}t & -8 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060147 1071 "Given that the minor of ${p}_{23}$ is ${t}^{2}+12$, find all the possible values of $t$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060147 1074 "First, we remove the second row and third column:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 4 \\\\ -1 & 9 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${p}_{23}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}t & -8 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =t\\cdot 9-(-8)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =9t-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${p}_{23}$ is ${t}^{2}+12$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9t-8 & ={t}^{2}+12 \\\\ {t}^{2}-9t+20 & =0 \\\\ (t-5)(t-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=4$ and $t=5.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057289 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401749 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060156 1071 "$\\,$ Either you do not water the plants, or they will grow.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060156 1071 "Either you do not water the plants, or they will grow.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060156 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You do not water the plants.\n\n- $B:$ The plants will grow.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You water the plants.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you water the plants, then they will grow.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ If you water the plants, they will grow.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058260 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402375 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060278 1071 "The angle $\\theta =-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060278 1071 "The angle $θ =-\\frac{9π}{4}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060278 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/89/891959642b0d055d852329c59be7541179dd5ae0c4182c2bec533261ce3a700d.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ also lies in this quadrant." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058806 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403205 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060369 1074 "![](images/02/0204cfb247273efc20a31366dcb84096d630aa6dbe90c94a8802834205bbdd56.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-sin⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-sin⁡x.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058998 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403495 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060401 1071 "What would be the system of equations for the following augmented matrix?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 10 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060401 1071 "What would be the system of equations for the following augmented matrix?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 10 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060401 1074 "The augmented matrix consists of two parts: the coefficient matrix and the column of constants.\n\nThe coefficient matrix represents the coefficients in the linear system, while the column of constants represents the constants on the right-hand sides of the equations.\n\nUsing the variables $x$ and  $y$,  the corresponding system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=10 \\\\ -x+y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186059112 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404204 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060420 1071 "Find the contrapositive of the following statement:\n\n**If** it is not warm outside, **then** you need a jacket." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060420 1071 "Find the contrapositive of the following statement: \n\n$\\,$ **If** it is not warm outside, **then** you need a jacket." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060420 1074 "In our conditional statement: \n\n- The antecedent is $A:$ It is not warm outside.\n\n- The consequent is $B:$ You need a jacket.\n\nThe contrapositive of a conditional statement $A⇒B$ is given by \n\n$$\n¬B⇒¬A.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the negations of $A$ and $B:$\n\n- $¬A:$ It is warm outside.\n\n- $¬B:$ You don't need a jacket.\n\nTherefore, the contrapositive is the following: \n\n$\\,$ If you don't need a jacket, then it is warm outside." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402908 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402915 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064277 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402990 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403639 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403646 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400176 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402970 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402977 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403925 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403036 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400148 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064293 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400183 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400950 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400957 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401818 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401825 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064378 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402956 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064388 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399397 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064388 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401941 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062293 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186064711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401948 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064711 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064711 1071 "A rocket that is launched vertically is tracked by a radar station located on\nthe ground $3\\,\\text{km}$ from the launch site. What is the vertical speed of the rocket at the instant when its distance from the radar station is $5\\,\\text{km}$ and this distance is increasing at a rate of $1\\,000\\,\\text{km/h}?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064711 1071 "A rocket that is launched vertically is tracked by a radar station located on\nthe ground $3km$ from the launch site. What is the vertical speed of the rocket at the instant when its distance from the radar station is $5km$ and this distance is increasing at a rate of $1000km/h$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186064711 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/7a/7af033f137fd76e5e3003926a598e695f4e0852b11dc27dfa84d58040144972b.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the radar station and the launch point,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the launch point and the rocket, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the radar station and the rocket.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=3\\,\\text{km}$ and $z=5\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $y$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,000\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- the distance between the radar station and the launch point ($x$) remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(3)(0)+2(4)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(5)(1\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{10\\,000}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\,250\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical speed of the rocket is $1\\,250\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063390 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186064894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403024 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064894 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x =-1$?\n$f(x) = {x^{3} + x,\\begin{vmatrix}x < - 1 \\\\ x^{2} + 1,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge - 1$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186064894 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+x, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064894 1074 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}({x}^{3}+x) \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1) \\\\  & =-2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063402 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186064896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403030 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064896 1071 "![](images/23/237c36808ded541ed264826866db3d2774dc2d5212f1292f21a6c25b3b07d0e1.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = f(x)$, defined on $[- 3, 5]$. Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186064896 1071 "![](images/23/237c36808ded541ed264826866db3d2774dc2d5212f1292f21a6c25b3b07d0e1.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064896 1074 "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downward like a frown ($⌢$) over the interval $(1,3).$\n\n![](images/a7/a7d9436275984c12271f2c737eb005e1eaab5b308c2204040e7aff5e87faffb3.png)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186068831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400922 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186068866 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399745 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186068866 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401967 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186068867 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399752 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186068881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400936 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186068882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400943 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186068906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401599 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186068906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401921 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186068906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403467 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186068906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403598 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186069223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066490 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186069223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401961 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186069223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186069223 1071 "The equation $x^{2} - 4x + 4 = 9$ can be written in the form $(x + p)^{2} = q$. Find $p + q$, and the sum of the roots $x_{1} + x_{2}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186069223 1071 "The equation ${x}^{2}-4x+4=9$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,p+q,$ and the sum of the roots ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186069223 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+4 & =9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot x & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2})-4 & =5 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-2,q=9,$ and $p+q=7.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2+3=5 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2-3=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=5.$ Thus, the sum is $4.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186069274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066784 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186069274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402362 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186069274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186069274 1071 "The equation $z^{2} - 50 =-10z$ can be written in the form $(z + p)^{2} = q$. Find $p + q$, and solve the equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186069274 1071 "The equation ${z}^{2}-50=-10z$ can be written in the form $(z+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186069274 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $-10z$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-50 & =-10z \\\\ {z}^{2}+10z & =50 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z & =50 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2} & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=5,q=75,$ and $p+q=80.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2} & =75 \\\\ \\sqrt{(z+5{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\ |z+5| & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\ z+5 & =\\pm 5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ z & =-5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186069340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067180 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186069340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402756 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186069340 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186069340 1071 "If the line $y =-2x + 3$ and the curve $y = \\frac{2}{x - 2} + 1$ intersect at the point $(p, q)$, then $p + q =$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186069340 1071 "If the line $y=-2x+3$ and the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1$ intersect at the point $(p,q),$ then $p+q=$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186069340 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x+3 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & =\\frac{2}{x-2}+1 \\\\ -2x+2 & =\\frac{2}{x-2} \\\\ (-2x+2)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ 2(-x+1)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ (-x+1)(x-2) & =1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4(1)(3)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{9-12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-2x+3$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41fee04552a47d93f1dbd788e69e12c4b58e377ec4c0b63c3ed55c712d337f94.png)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078397 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399418 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078397 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403842 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403835 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078489 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403543 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078497 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402729 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078499 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400155 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078499 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402722 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078500 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403164 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078500 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404502 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078502 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404509 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078574 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399863 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078574 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399884 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078574 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402963 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078589 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399586 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186079202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186077960 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186079202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403550 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186079202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186079202 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨6t-2,4⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0,0)$ and has velocity $⟨1,\\,4⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186079202 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$a(t) = ⟨6t - 2, 4⟩$.\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0, 0)$ and has velocity $⟨1, 4⟩$ when $t = 1$, calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186079202 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨6t-2,4⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (6t-2)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-2t+{C}_{1},\\,4t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨1,4⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨3(1)^{2}-2(1)+{C}_{1},\\,4(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,4⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},4+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨3{t}^{2}-2t,\\,4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3{t}^{2}-2t,\\,4t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (3{t}^{2}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+{C}_{3},\\,2{t}^{2}+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(1)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}+{C}_{3},\\,2(1)^{2}+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{3},2+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=0$ and ${C}_{4}=-2.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083207 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399356 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401846 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401859 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403278 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403285 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083249 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401068 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083249 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401075 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083251 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400071 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083251 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403828 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083278 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400309 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083278 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400316 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400323 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083301 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400582 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083301 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404973 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400589 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404980 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401061 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083313 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399780 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400680 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400687 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083335 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404987 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083335 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404994 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083335 1038 #edb/ref 17592186405001 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399460 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400715 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080747 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186083619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401866 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083619 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+7,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083619 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = 4\\sin t + 7, y = 4\\cos t + 10, 0 \\le t < 2π$.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186083619 1074 "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087662 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399939 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087662 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399946 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087666 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401082 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087668 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399981 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087668 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403064 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403292 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403299 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400218 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400211 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403306 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087704 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400225 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400330 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403515 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403522 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400190 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403334 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400120 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403681 13