e] [17592186087877 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404571 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087877 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404578 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403130 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404585 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404599 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404272 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402901 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186084932 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186088038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401439 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088038 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088038 1071 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088038 1071 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n$2\\frac{dy}{dx} - 3\\cos x = 1 - 4y\\sin x$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186088038 1074 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085998 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186088229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403112 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088229 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 4\\frac{dy}{dx} - 12y = 12x^{2} - 32x - 30$\nhas the complementary function $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{2x} + Be^{-6x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186088229 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-6x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088229 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-12\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha -12\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-12\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha -12\\beta =-32\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma =-30\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =2,\\gamma =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+3.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086016 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186088232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403124 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088232 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 2\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = 10e^{x}$\nhas the complementary function $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{x} + Bxe^{x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186088232 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=10{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088232 1074 "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x},$ we have that $\\lambda =1$ is the double root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10{e}^{x}.$  In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $1$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha ({x}^{2}+4x+2){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha ({x}^{2}+4x+2){e}^{x}-2(\\alpha ({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{x})+(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{x}) & =10{e}^{x} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+4x+2-2{x}^{2}-4x+{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{x} & =10{e}^{x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{x} & =10{e}^{x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =10 \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{x}^{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087407 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186088464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404726 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088464 1071 "Determine the points where the ellipse $3x^{2} + y^{2} - 6\\sqrt{3}x - 6y + 6 = 0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186088464 1071 "Determine the points where the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6\\sqrt{3}x-6y+6=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088464 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6\\sqrt{3}x-6y+6 & =0 \\\\ 3(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-6\\sqrt{3}(0)-6y+6 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-6y+6 & =0 \\\\ (y-3)^{2}-9+6 & =0 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =3 \\\\ y-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =3\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,3-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,3+\\sqrt{3}).$\n\n![](images/de/de18c7e39dd73d5f72fd32b34b97b65cf0011e6da44b36a1d4b3d62df8852a9a.png)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399918 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403953 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403960 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402508 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402515 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399607 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092243 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400141 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092282 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400239 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092282 1038 #edb/ref 17592186405008 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092379 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399411 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092379 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401020 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092379 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401027 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400078 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401040 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400085 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400092 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401047 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401054 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401872 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089603 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186092577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401034 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092577 1071 "![](images/4b/4b03822d65ec9f5bea3a1e47d25e4831470754fa09cef510e7c452cc292e9e40.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r = θe^{θ}$ for $0 \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186092577 1071 "![](images/4b/4b03822d65ec9f5bea3a1e47d25e4831470754fa09cef510e7c452cc292e9e40.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092577 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={e}^{\\theta }+\\theta {e}^{\\theta }=(1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\theta {e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{((1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }+(1+2\\theta +{\\theta }^{2}){e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1){e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186090604 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186092739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402984 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092739 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092739 1071 "If $f(x) = 3^{2x^{2}}$, find $f^{′}(x)$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186092739 1071 "If $f(x)={3}^{2{x}^{2}},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092739 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={3}^{u},\\,u=2{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={3}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={3}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\cdot (4x) \\\\  & =4x\\cdot {3}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\  & =4x\\cdot {3}^{2{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096620 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401633 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096622 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403418 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096630 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399836 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096630 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399843 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096630 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401980 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096630 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403973 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096630 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403980 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403884 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400519 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403660 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399995 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096712 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403709 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096713 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403702 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096714 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404022 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096730 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404398 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096730 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404405 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096734 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404391 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400099 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096749 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400106 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403779 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403786 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400624 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096790 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401804 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096791 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401914 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186093824 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186096980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399850 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096980 1071 "![](images/27/27f92e7288aa52ef67a6346fc5101173f51993be77cdd8face0517f2e6e790d3.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$. The graph $y = g^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186096980 1071 "![](images/27/27f92e7288aa52ef67a6346fc5101173f51993be77cdd8face0517f2e6e790d3.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096980 1074 "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ does not cross the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ no inflection points." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186097224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095243 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186097224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401974 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186097224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186097224 1071 "![](images/19/19e9763d62ca7818024bf49522a127a48dd04f76d1175c93e060a1043b6a5795.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$. The graph $y = f^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186097224 1071 "![](images/19/19e9763d62ca7818024bf49522a127a48dd04f76d1175c93e060a1043b6a5795.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186097224 1074 "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=0$ and $x=4.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=0$ and $x=4.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186097356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186096030 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186097356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403633 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186097356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186097356 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186097356 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n$M = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186097356 1074 "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the $2$nd row by $-\\frac{1}{3}$ and the $3$rd row by $\\frac{1}{2}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186100822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401893 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186100840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399425 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186100877 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403891 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186100900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404237 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186100928 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404071 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186100929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404057 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186100985 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402501 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186100995 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400197 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186100996 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400204 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186100996 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400708 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101001 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399891 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101029 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400002 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098819 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186101253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401654 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101253 1071 "Find the general solution to the following equation.\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{y + 2x^{2}y^{2}}{x}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z = y^{-1}$.*" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186101253 1071 "Find the general solution to the following equation. \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{x}\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101253 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1}\\,\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{z}^{-1}+2{x}^{2}{z}^{-2}}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{xz}+\\frac{2x}{{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{z}{x}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{z}{x}-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-2x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-2x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-2{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int 2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{3}}{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{3}}{3x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3x}{C-2{x}^{3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098849 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186101258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401660 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101258 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + (y(y - 1))/(x) = 0, y(1) = 5, x > 0$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n$\\frac{dz}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}z = \\frac{1}{x}$\nusing the substitution $z = y^{-1}$.*" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186101258 1071 "Solve the initial value problem \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y(y-1)}{x}=0,\\,y(1)=5,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nusing the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101258 1074 "The substitution $z={y}^{-1}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =x+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{x+C}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{x+C}{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now determine $C$ from the initial condition  $y(1)=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =\\frac{1}{1+C}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{(x-\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x}{(5x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099621 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186101415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402816 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101415 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 1 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -0 & 6\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statement are true regarding the matrices $A,B,$ and $C?$\n\nI. The product $AB$ is well defined\nII. The product $BA$ is well defined\nIII. The product $AC$ is well defined" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101415 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 1 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$,\n\nWhich of the following statement are true regarding the matrices $A, B$, and $C$?\n\n1. The product $AB$ is well defined\n2. The product $BA$ is well defined\n3. The product $AC$ is well defined" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186101415 1074 "Notice that $A$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThere are $2$ columns in $A$ and $2$ rows in $B.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $B,$ the matrices $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 4}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $4$ columns in $B$ and $4$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ equals the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{B}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\times }_{✓}{A}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $2$ columns in $A$ and $2$ rows in $C.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $C,$ the matrices $A$ and $C$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{C}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that all three statements are true." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105716 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399822 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105716 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403010 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105718 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400666 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105718 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400673 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402997 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402788 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403695 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400603 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402574 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400610 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400617 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402594 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404176 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105774 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399544 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403737 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105863 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402408 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105863 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402681 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105877 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399967 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105878 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404432 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401232 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401239 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105904 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400281 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105904 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403877 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105914 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400344 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186106074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102300 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186106074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401322 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186106074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186106074 1071 "![](images/e2/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186106074 1071 "![](images/e2/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186106074 1074 "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186106155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102776 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186106155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401853 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186106155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186106155 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n$M = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186106155 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186106155 1074 "The system corresponding to $M$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y+4z=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\nx-y+4z=-1\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y,z.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\nx=-1+y-4z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+y-4z \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$, $z$ (free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1+y-4z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186110577 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399925 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186110577 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399932 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186110581 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403383 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186110593 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399960 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186110593 1038