2186401459 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401508 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401515 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401522 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401529 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401536 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124661 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399474 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124661 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399481 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124662 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404356 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124662 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404377 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124663 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401585 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124663 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404370 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124664 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404258 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124664 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404363 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402139 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402146 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402153 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121247 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399733 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124780 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124780 1071 "The migration table below describes the movement of the population between cities and suburbs every year in a certain state.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Cities** | **Suburbs** |\n| To: | **Cities** | $0.8$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Suburbs** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |  |\n\nThat is, $20%$ of those living in cities move to the suburbs each year, and $70%$ of those living in suburbs move to the cities.\n\nAssuming that the population of the state is $9$ million and remains constant, how many people would eventually live in cities if the given probabilities remain constant over many years?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124780 1071 "The migration table below describes the movement of the population between cities and suburbs every year in a certain state. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Cities** | **Suburbs** |\n| To: | **Cities** | $0.8$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Suburbs** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |  |\n\nThat is, $20%$ of those living in cities move to the suburbs each year, and $70%$ of those living in suburbs move to the cities.\n\nAssuming that the population of the state is $9$ million and remains constant, how many people would eventually live in cities if the given probabilities remain constant over many years?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124780 1074 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{7}{9}$ of the $9$ million population will live in cities. Therefore, the total number of people living in cities after many years will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot 9\\,\\text{million}=7\\,\\text{million}.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121487 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401391 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124820 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124820 1071 "![](images/b6/b698fe735caaf3bc260c1e921bb95596df8869eba67ec9ce72484b4d86cc8c69.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z = f(x, y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124820 1071 "![](images/b6/b698fe735caaf3bc260c1e921bb95596df8869eba67ec9ce72484b4d86cc8c69.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124820 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A$ and $D.$ Hence $A$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D({x}_{D},{y}_{D},{z}_{D}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{D},{y}_{D})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](images/0a/0a371aa559222bd28c37677bace18eb912bdc978e364bab4cb3d83f7a310de3f.png)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121880 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402402 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124885 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic cylinder $9x^{2} + 2(y - 1)^{2} = 18$ and the plane $z = 1$ onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124885 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic cylinder $9{x}^{2}+2(y-1)^{2}=18$ and the plane $z=1$ onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124885 1074 "To find the intersection of the cylinder and the plane $z=1,$ we substitute $z=1$ into the equation of the cylinder. However, since the equation has no (explicit) $z$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+2(y-1)^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{(3)^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121898 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402415 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124888 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{x^{2}}{8} + ((y - 4)^{2})/(18) - \\frac{z^{2}}{50} = 1$ and the plane $x = 2$ onto the $yz$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124888 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50}=1$ and the plane $x=2$ onto the $yz$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124888 1074 "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $x=2$, we substitute $x=2$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50} & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $3$ and $5.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121904 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402421 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124889 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $((x - 2)^{2})/(4) + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} - \\frac{z^{2}}{5} = 1$, find its $x$-intercepts." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124889 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5}=1$, find its $x$-intercepts." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124889 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $x=0$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121922 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402427 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124892 1071 "Given the ellipsoid $((x - 2)^{2})/(12) + ((y + 1)^{2})/(6) + ((z - 1)^{2})/(2) = 1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124892 1071 "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{2}=1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124892 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $x=0$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122036 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402461 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124911 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124911 1071 "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A$, given by\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124911 1071 "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124911 1074 "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{4} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122300 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402488 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124955 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124955 1071 "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=5 \\\\ y=4t \\\\ z=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat would be the vector equation of the line?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124955 1071 "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n${x = 5; y = 4t; z = 7$\nWhat would be the vector equation of the line?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124955 1074 "These parametric equations are equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4t \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125054 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122894 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125054 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402536 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125054 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125054 1071 "Find the eigenvectors of the $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $E$ given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}E = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125054 1071 "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]E=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125054 1074 "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E:$\n\n$$\nE={{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125056 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122906 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125056 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402542 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125056 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125056 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125056 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-2$, $λ_{2} =-1$, and $λ_{3} = 1$.\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A = PDP^{-1}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125056 1074 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122990 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402548 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125070 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} + y^{2} + 6xy + 2$ on the closed region $D = {(x, y): x^{2} + y^{2} \\le 2}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125070 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6xy+2$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125070 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+6y & 2y+6x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x+6y=0 \\\\ 2y+6x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+6(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2 \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2 \\\\  & =4+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{2},0),\\,(1,1),\\,(-1,1),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\sqrt{2},0)$ | $(1,1)$ | $(-1,1)$ | $(-1,-1)$ | $(1,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $10$ | $-2$ | $10$ | $-2$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $10$ at the points $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123158 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402568 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125098 1071 "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = B$, where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125098 1071 "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125098 1074 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )-8\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186124034 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404638 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125244 1071 "Given the elliptic cone $((x - 2)^{2})/(2) + ((y + 2)^{2})/(4) = (z + 3)^{2}$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125244 1071 "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=(z+3)^{2},$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125244 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts of the elliptic cone, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{4} & =(0+3)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{4}{4} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+1 & =9 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2} & =8 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(-2,0,0)$ and $(6,0,0).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186124166 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404946 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125266 1071 "Let $f(x, y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $AB^(―)$, where $a = ⟨1, 2⟩$ and $b = ⟨2, 3⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B$, respectively. If $f(a) = 1$ and $f(b) = 6$, then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C ∈ AB^(―)$ with position vector $c$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125266 1071 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=1$ and $f(b)=6,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125266 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ 6-1 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨2,3⟩-⟨1,2⟩) \\\\ 5 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 5 & ={f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129088 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400162 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129088 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404321 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129089 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400169 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129089 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404328 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402887 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402894 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402928 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402942 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402949 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129103 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399446 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129103 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399453 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129103 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402668 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129103 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402935 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129119 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400442 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129120 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400414 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129120 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400421 1319413953347