92186401013 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129259 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400915 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400985 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400992 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404029 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129276 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401281 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129276 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401288 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401328 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401335 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129312 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402073 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129312 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402080 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129328 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400880 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129328 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402641 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126026 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186400030 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129426 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129426 1071 "Consider the expression $f(x, y) = 2x^{2}y$. What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x, y)$?\n\n| $y > 0$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $y < 0$ |  |  |\n|  | $x < 0$ | $x > 0$ |" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129426 1071 "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n| $y>0$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129426 1074 "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/93/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $2{x}^{2}$ | $y$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/7c/7c851189a040934728e1b312706b4727c141cc678a4be870b6057e281be6e0fd.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126392 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186400743 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129487 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129487 1071 "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(3i + 2j)m/s^{2}$. The velocity of the particle is $(-7i - 6j)m/s$ when $t = 0s$. Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $2im/s$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129487 1071 "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(3i+2j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ The velocity of the particle is $(-7i-6j)\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$ Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $2i\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129487 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\na=3i+2j,\\,u=-7i-6j,\\,v=2i\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 2i & =(-7i-6j)+(3i+2j)t \\\\ 9i+6j & =(3i+2j)t \\\\ 9i+6j & =3ti+2tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}9=3t \\\\ 6=2t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126661 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401295 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129532 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T$, where $T$ is defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} - w^{2} \\\\ we^{u} \\\\ u - v\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129532 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-{w}^{2} \\\\ w{e}^{u} \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129532 1074 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}-{w}^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=w{e}^{u},\\,z(u,v,w)=u-v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-2w \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =w{e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 & -2w \\\\ w{e}^{u} & 0 & {e}^{u} \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u|\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{u} \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2w|\\begin{aligned}w{e}^{u} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u(0+{e}^{u})-2w(-w{e}^{u}-0) \\\\  & =2u{e}^{u}+2{w}^{2}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2{e}^{u}({w}^{2}+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2{e}^{u}({w}^{2}+u) & =0 \\\\ {w}^{2}+u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${w}^{2}+u=0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $u=-{w}^{2}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126969 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402029 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129586 1071 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) + 3x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)$.\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1} =-2$ and $λ_{2} = 2$.*" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129586 1071 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$*" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129586 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126975 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402035 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129587 1071 "Find the substitution that will decouple the following system of differential equations:\n\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 3x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = x_{2}(t)$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129587 1071 "Find the substitution that will decouple the following system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129587 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we have $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $x(t)=Eu(t)$, or equivalently,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127231 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402588 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129630 1071 "At which points on the surface $z = 3xy - \\ln y$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector $v = ⟨3, 5,-1⟩$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129630 1071 "At which points on the surface $z=3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨3,5,-1⟩?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129630 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3x-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨3,5,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩=⟨3,5,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3cy=3 \\\\ 3cx-\\frac{c}{y}=5 \\\\ -c=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3(1)y=3\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ and $y=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n3(1)x-\\frac{1}{1}=5\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =3(2)(1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(2,1,6).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127315 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402629 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129644 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129644 1071 "A skater is skating straight down a ramp. The displacement $D$ of the skater increases at a rate that's proportional to the square of the time $t$, in seconds.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of displacement $D$ at time $t$, where $t$ is the time since he started skating?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129644 1071 "A skater is skating straight down a ramp. The displacement $D$ of the skater increases at a rate that's proportional to the square of the time $t,$ in seconds.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of displacement $D$ at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time since he started skating?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129644 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*A skater is skating straight down a ramp. The **displacement** $D$ of the skater **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t,$ in seconds. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *displacement* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *square of the time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}∝{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the displacement *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127567 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402662 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129686 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129686 1074 "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\n- the $y$-intercept is negative, which means that $P(0)<0.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, only $P(x)=-{x}^{3}+2x-1$ satisties all these conditions." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127759 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402675 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129718 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129718 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129718 1071 "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A = PCP^{-1}$, what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129718 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 5\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=5$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =5+(-2)\\text{i}=5-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1+4\\text{i} \\\\ 3+0\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1+4\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =5∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129760 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128011 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129760 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402688 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129760 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129760 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T$, where $T$ is defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} - v \\\\ u^{2} + v \\\\ e^{w}\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129760 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ {u}^{2}+v \\\\ {e}^{w}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129760 1074 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}-v,\\,y(u,v,w)={u}^{2}+v,\\,z(u,v,w)={e}^{w}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & ={e}^{w}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {e}^{w}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{w}|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{w}(2u+2u) \\\\  & =4u{e}^{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 4u{e}^{w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128137 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402776 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129781 1071 "![](images/91/919b9e8f2a9493fd0dda3e0d7bf66de9af054fc4ed9c0fcf7a68329fdbbaffdd.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin θ = \\frac{1}{2}$, what is the value of $\\tan θ$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129781 1071 "![](images/91/919b9e8f2a9493fd0dda3e0d7bf66de9af054fc4ed9c0fcf7a68329fdbbaffdd.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2},$ what is the value of $tan⁡\\theta ?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129781 1074 "![](images/94/948610f67267e6ec1b845d2b4e1bc4030b81a1080bcb9a44d9f974ff78c3f7b0.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128281 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402922 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129805 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129805 1071 "![](images/f1/f1e1a140e78362efddf48ba6744e2f88d109000fba8eae97818b024c2d249ad5.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y = f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129805 1071 "![](images/f1/f1e1a140e78362efddf48ba6744e2f88d109000fba8eae97818b024c2d249ad5.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129805 1074 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nIn the same way, the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(1)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but this is not a jump discontinuity." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403932 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403939 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133767 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403765 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400832 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402742 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402749 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133812 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400818 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133812 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400825 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133814 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400845 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133814 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401136 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133814 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402795 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400852 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400859 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400866 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133821 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403758 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400783 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400790 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400804 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400811 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133911 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400797 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133912 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401096 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133912 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401103 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133917 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400873 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133918 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402867 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133918 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402874 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401342 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133962 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399495 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186134112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130798 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186134112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186400763 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186134112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186134112 1071 "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-5$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186134112 1071 "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-5$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the line $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1}\\end{bmatrix} = (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y$,\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186134112 1074 "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-5$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186134117 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130828 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186134117 1068 #edb/ref 17592186400839 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186134117 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186134117 1071 "A biologist has recorded data on a pine tree's age (in years) and height (in feet). This data is shown below.\n\n| Age $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Height $(y)$ | $1.2$ | $2.5$ | $3.6$ | $4.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the tree's height after $8$ years, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n$(X^{T}X)^{-1} = \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, X^{T}y = \\begin{bmatrix}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix}$,\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186134117 1071 "A biologist has recorded data on a pine tree's age (in years) and height (in feet). This data is shown below. \n\n| Age $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Height $(y)$ | $1.2$ | $2.5$ | $3.6$ | $4.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the tree's height after $8$ years, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186134117 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1.2 \\\\ 2.5 \\\\ 3.6 \\\\ 4.5\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 1.1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.2+1.1x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the  height after $8$ years, we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.2+1.1(8) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the model, the height of a pine tree that is $8$ years old will be $9$ feet. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186134314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131893 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186134314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402736 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186134314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186134314 1071 "![](images/0e/0e05b575c22c78000a1ec235bb82cbfad3cd0cdfc6db370f19f094799b305949.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y = 2x^{2}$ and $y = 1 + x^{2}$, as shown above, then $∬_(D)f(x, y)dA =$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186134314 1071 "![](images/0e/0e05b575c22c78000a1ec235bb82cbfad3cd0cdfc6db370f19f094799b305949.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=2{x}^{2}$ and $y=1+{x}^{2},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186134314 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/da/da2895c09bc33f628626257b6d102e74875825df14ba744904e02ffa71552a02.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{1+{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{1+{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186138484 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399649 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186138484 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403071 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403098 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147405 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400901 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147431 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400435 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147436 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404678 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147436 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404685 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147446 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404078 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147446 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404085 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404692 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147460 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399663 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147505 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404488 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147513 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404036 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147516 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402381 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147520 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403137 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147521 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404460 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147596 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404043 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147598 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403105 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147598 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404495 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186148130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146504 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186148130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403118 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186148130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186148130 1071 "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8$\nII. $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2$\nIII. $2x+7$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186148130 1071 "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$P(x, y)dx + 4y^{3}dy = 0$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x, y)$?\n\n1. $\\cos (x) + 8$\n2. $\\ln (x + 1) - 2$\n3. $2x + 7$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186148130 1074 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=4{y}^{3}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{3})=0\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2x+7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+7)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2x+7$ gives an exact equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186148153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146642 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186148153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403144 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186148153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186148153 1071 "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s) = \\frac{6}{s^{2} - 6s}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186148153 1071 "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s)=\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-6s}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186148153 1074 "First, we write $F(s)$ as a sum of partial fractions, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-6s}=\\frac{6}{s(s-6)}=\\frac{1}{s-6}-\\frac{1}{s}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{6t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-6},\\,s>6 \\\\ L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition property of the Laplace transform, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-6}-\\frac{1}{s}} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-6}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-6s}$ is $f(t)={e}^{6t}-1.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401666 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401673 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152086 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401626 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152086 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401879 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152088 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401619 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152088 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401783 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401762 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401769 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401776 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401886 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401790 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401797 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400568 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400575 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404064 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399516 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403320 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403987 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399537 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403994 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404001 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404008 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404015 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152282 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400561 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152282 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402248 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152282 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402255 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152287 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402213 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152287 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403178 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152288 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402220 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400449 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149129 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186152438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401123 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152438 1071 "![](images/0c/0c46972a3968d7110cb08d4b16bfff9267e49b102cf005cdbb21a3630d15c49d.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $2$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $θ ∈ [0, 2π)$, traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7, 0)$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186152438 1071 "![](images/0c/0c46972a3968d7110cb08d4b16bfff9267e49b102cf005cdbb21a3630d15c49d.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $2,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,0)?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152438 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=7$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151163 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186152781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403905 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152781 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point $S^{′}$, the image of $S(-4, \\sqrt{3})$ under the action of $T$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186152781 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point ${S}^{′}$, the image of $S(-4,\\sqrt{3})$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152781 1074 "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a column-vector $x$ containing the coordinates of $S:$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $x$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $Tx:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $Tx,$ contains the coordinates of ${S}^{′}.$ Therefore, ${S}^{′}(15,-1).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152802 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151289 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186152802 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403967 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152802 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152802 1071 "![](images/14/14d1fc5a92c28cc7f84c1ec23929701f1aca67b1a15d6113fa83f1428354fc11.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,-1)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $3$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $θ ∈ [0, 2π)$, traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,-1)$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186152802 1071 "![](images/14/14d1fc5a92c28cc7f84c1ec23929701f1aca67b1a15d6113fa83f1428354fc11.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,-1)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,-1)?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152802 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,-1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151745 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186152878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404612 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152878 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152878 1071 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n1. $(x - 1)^{2}y^{″} + (x - 1)y^{′} + y = 0$\n2. $8y^{″} + y^{′} + y = 0, y(1) = 0, y^{′}(2) = 10$\n3. $x^{2}y^{″} + xy^{′} = x^{2} + 1, y(1) = 0, y^{′}(1) = 5$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186152878 1071 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nI. $(x-1)^{2}{y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y=0$\nII. $8{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=10$\nIII. ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+1,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152878 1074 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$)." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404453 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157247 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404230 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401900 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157253 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401199 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157254 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401907 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157334 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404439 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157437 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404425 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157438 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404418 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155859 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186157828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404224 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157828 1074 "First, we simplify the phrase \"the pen is blue and it is a blue pen\" to just \"the pen is blue.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The pen is blue, or the pen is blue or it is a red pen.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma in the sentence:\n\n\"The pen is blue or the pen is blue, or it is a red pen.\"\n\nNow, we simplify the phrase \"the pen is blue or the pen is blue\" to just \"the pen is blue.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The pen is blue, or it is a red pen.\"\n\nFinally, we simplify the sentence to\n\n\"The pen is blue or red.\"" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156399 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186157918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404412 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157918 1071 "Solve the inequality $3^{4x}x^{2} \\ge 3^{4x + 1}x$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186157918 1071 "Solve the inequality ${3}^{4x}{x}^{2}\\ge {3}^{4x+1}x.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157918 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4x}{x}^{2} & \\ge {3}^{4x+1}x \\\\ {3}^{4x}{x}^{2}-{3}^{4x+1}x & \\ge 0 \\\\ {3}^{4x}({x}^{2}-3x) & \\ge 0 \\\\ {3}^{4x}x(x-3) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${3}^{4x}$ and get\n\n$$\nx(x-3)\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis represents an upwards parabola whose roots are at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ The parabola is at or above the $x$-axis when $x\\le 0$ or $x\\ge 3.$\n\n![](images/3b/3bfb82543e7c05d0ca0b8f6c6973025400ad1253acbc57214173dbd3e8c37fef.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is  $x\\le 0$ or $x\\ge 3.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186161971 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400484 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186161971 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402847 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186161971 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404536 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186161972 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400498 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186161972 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402834 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186161972 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404543 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186161973 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400477 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186161973 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404516 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186161973 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404523 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186161995 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404699 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186161995 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404706 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162001 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404644 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162002 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404657 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162002 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404664 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162002 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404671 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162012 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400908 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162012 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404772 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162017 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404828 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162017 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404835 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162018 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404751 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162018 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404758 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162018 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404765 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162025 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404842 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162025 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404897 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162104 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400769 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162104 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400776 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162110 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400971 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162110 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400978 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160616 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186162559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402828 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162559 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162559 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form $R_{i}:=R_{i} + kR_{3}$, the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B$. What are the elementary row operations?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186162559 1074 "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries above ${a}_{32}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{3}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160628 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186162561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402841 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162561 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162561 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -7 & -4 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations \n\nI. ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+{C}_{3}$\nII. ${C}_{2}:=…$\nIII. ${C}_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162561 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -7 & -4 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations\n\n1. $C_{1}:=C_{1} + C_{3}$\n2. $C_{2}:=…$\n3. $C_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form $C_{i}:=C_{i} + kC_{3}$, the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B$. What are the missing elementary column operations?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186162561 1074 "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{33}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -7 & -4 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -7 & -4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-3{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-2{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-3{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-2{C}_{3}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160634 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186162562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402854 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162562 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162562 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162562 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form $C_{i}:=C_{i} + kC_{3}$, the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B$. What are the elementary column operations?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186162562 1074 "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left of ${a}_{33}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-3{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-3{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{3}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160718 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186162576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404530 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162576 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -9\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{2},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162576 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form $R_{i}:=R_{i} + kR_{2}$, the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B$. What are the elementary row operations?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186162576 1074 "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries above and below ${a}_{23}=-3$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160940 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186162613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404606 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162613 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162613 1071 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x) = 2x_{1}^{2} - 4x_{1}x_{2} - x_{2}^{2}$ to the canonical form given below.\n\n$2y_{1}^{2} - 3y_{2}^{2}, {y_{1} = x_{1} - x_{2}; y_{2} = x_{2}$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186162613 1071 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n2{y}_{1}^{2}-\\boxed{}\\,{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162613 1074 "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{1}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2})-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})-{x}_{2}^{2} & =2([{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}]-{x}_{2}^{2})-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})-{x}_{2}^{2} & =2({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ $3\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161018 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186162626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404651 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162626 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162626 1071 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x) = 3x_{1}^{2} + 29x_{2}^{2} + 18x_{1}x_{2}$ to the canonical form given below.\n\n$3y_{1}^{2} + 2y_{2}^{2}, {y_{1} = x_{1} + 3x_{2}; y_{2} = x_{2}$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186162626 1071 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+29{x}_{2}^{2}+18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162626 1074 "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{1}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+29{x}_{2}^{2}+18{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =(3{x}_{1}^{2}+18{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} & =3([{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}]-9{x}_{2}^{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3(({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} & =3({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}-27{x}_{2}^{2}+29{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ $3\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161090 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186162638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404713 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162638 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162638 1071 "Given the quadratic form $Q(x) =-x_{1}^{2} - 4x_{1}x_{2} - x_{2}^{2}$, find its canonical (diagonal) form $y^{T}Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x = Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186162638 1071 "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162638 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404821 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401178 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404849 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404904 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404911 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404856 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404884 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166865 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399904 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166868 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400064 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401225 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399684 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166935 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402174 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402290 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402308 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402326 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402344 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186167091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163594 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186167091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399898 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186167091 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186167091 1071 "![](images/1b/1b608fabe99c6062f9d9f04cc7f3b2933e453812ed4ce9783908666c71c1c74b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = \\frac{y}{y - 1}$\n2. $y^{′} = \\frac{1}{y}$\n3. $y^{′} = \\frac{1}{y^{2}}$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186167091 1071 "![](images/1b/1b608fabe99c6062f9d9f04cc7f3b2933e453812ed4ce9783908666c71c1c74b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$\nII. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\nIII. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186167091 1074 "![](images/7b/7bb52825944a7515a6281a88b5bc740cf6d097380fa13fe7e00efc5e499d49f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive everywhere.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{y-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y<0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 0.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for all $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186167132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163840 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186167132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401212 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186167132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186167132 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{\\boxed{}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186167132 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $n^(~)$, where\n\n$F(x, y, z) = ⟨z, y, x⟩, n^(~)(x, y, z) = ⟨2y,-z, 2x⟩, n = (n^(~))/(‖n^(~)‖)$.\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(S)\\frac{AAAAAAA}{\\sqrt{4x^{2} + 4y^{2} + z^{2}}}dS$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186167132 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{(2y)^{2}+(-z)^{2}+(2x)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2yz-yz+2{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+yz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}+yz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186167472 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166010 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186167472 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404891 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186167472 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186167472 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186167472 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186167472 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that from the matrix $Σ,$ we can deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$ without solving the characteristic equation.\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-6I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-0I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A{A}^{T}$): Since the first singular value is not zero, and we know the corresponding singular right vector, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$ \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172030 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399363 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172030 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404550 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172032 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404618 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172032 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404625 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172034 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404807 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172034 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404814 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172036 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400894 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172036 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404863 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172036 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404870 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172036 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404877 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172062 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401089 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172095 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400113 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172138 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401218 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172141 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401397 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172141 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401404 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169119 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186172381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401206 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172381 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172381 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=xyz$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,uv⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172381 1071 "$∬_(S)f(x, y, z)dS = ∬_(D)AAAAAAAdA$\nConsider the surface integral of the function\n$f(x, y, z) = xyz$\nover the parametric surface\n$r(u, v) = ⟨u, v, uv⟩$\nfor $(u, v) ∈ D⊆R^{2}$. What is the missing expression?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186172381 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 1 & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & v \\\\ 1 & u\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-v,\\,-u,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-v,\\,-u,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-v)^{2}+(-u)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(xyz)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=v\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =u\\cdot v\\cdot uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171327 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186172622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404632 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172622 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172622 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172622 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues $λ_{1} = 4$ and $λ_{2} =-2$. Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D = P^{T}AP$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186172622 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186175932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400023 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186175932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403536 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186175945 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403626 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186175954 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399390 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186175970 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399432 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186175982 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399439 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176001 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399705 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176001 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399712 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176016 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399759 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176059 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399404 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176072 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401301 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402227 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402234 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402241 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399670 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399677 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403508 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173338 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186176185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399384 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176185 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $r = 3$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186176185 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $r=3$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176185 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nr=3\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $z$-axis equals $3,$ while $\\theta $ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the circular cylinder shown below.\n\n![](images/aa/aa435c0ad4b4482c85a05af32afbbb7819b330a4292762ec66bad30a6ed31a44.png)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173944 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186176286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399739 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176286 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176286 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = PJP^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P$?\n\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, y = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, z = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186176286 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(0).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(0) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $x$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-0I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175138 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186176505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403004 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176505 1071 "The displacement $s$, in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t) = (3t - 5)^{2}$, where $t > 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186176505 1071 "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=(3t-5)^{2}$, where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176505 1074 "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((3t-5)^{2}) \\\\  & =6(3t-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ 6(3t-5) & <0 \\\\ 3t-5 & <0 \\\\ 3t & <5 \\\\ t & <\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (0,\\frac{5}{3}).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186179660 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400701 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186179707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400043 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186179707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400050 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186179707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400057 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186179707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400999 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186179782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401363 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184173 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399593 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184173 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402440 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184186 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400929 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184197 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399766 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399773 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403078 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403085 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404335 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404342 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404349 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184204 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404779 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184204 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404786 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184205 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404793 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184205 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404800 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184339 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400964 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184339 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403369 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184353 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400260 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400267 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400274 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401832 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404244 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184821 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183505 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186184821 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403092 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184821 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184821 1071 "The equation $2x^{2} - 4x - 9 = 0$ can be written in the form $2(x + p)^{2} = q$. Find $q$, and solve the equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186184821 1071 "The equation $2{x}^{2}-4x-9=0$ can be written in the form $2(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184821 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-4x-9 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x & =9 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-2x) & =9 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{(1)}^{2}-{(1)}^{2}] & =9 \\\\ 2[{(x-1)}^{2}-1] & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-1)}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 & =9 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2}-2 & =9 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2} & =9+2 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $q=11.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-1)}^{2} & =11 \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2} & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-1)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183877 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186184883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404739 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184883 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186184883 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184883 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4-\\lambda  \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-16)-4(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(20-4\\lambda -5\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-16)-4(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +4)-4(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +4-4) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183883 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186184884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404745 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184884 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186184884 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184884 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186188919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186187560 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186188919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402635 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186188919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186188919 1071 "The size of a bush growing in a schoolyard is being monitored. The area $A\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}$ of the bush at any given moment grows at a rate that's directly proportional to the area at that moment. Additionally, $2\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}$ of the bush is cut away every month.\nWhat is the differential equation that describes the rate of change in the area of the schoolyard bush at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time in months since it started growing?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186188919 1071 "The size of a bush growing in a schoolyard is being monitored. The area $Am^{2}$ of the bush at any given moment grows at a rate that's directly proportional to the area at that moment. Additionally, $2m^{2}$ of the bush is cut away every month.\nWhat is the differential equation that describes the rate of change in the area of the schoolyard bush at time $t$, where $t$ is the time in months since it started growing?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186188919 1074 "The rate of change in the area of the bush per month increases in proportion to $A$ and decreases by $2.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the (positive) constant of proportionality." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186192793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401274 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186193185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186190966 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186193185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401117 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186193185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186193185 1071 "![](images/b9/b95c538613ee8672e0f1cac970b7af230ec40bf3075e609b1e03f7cad4382fb1.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is\n$intA = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: 0 < x < 1, A A A A A}$.\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186193185 1071 "![](images/b9/b95c538613ee8672e0f1cac970b7af230ec40bf3075e609b1e03f7cad4382fb1.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<1,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186193185 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/19/19e8e52881066864cba562ed2a8cdc2944b9dae416c7124fdee207cdad1d8bdf.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/90/907e996fb7aea36e0c924feb0f5d9dc6804c768be775953d277e84b0145ef5a0.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<1,\\,-1<y<0}.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399988 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403017 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401680 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401687 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198267 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401755 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403432 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403446 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198360 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403723 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403800 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198366 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401185 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198366 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401192 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198366 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402622 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198366 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404162 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198366 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404169 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198385 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401267 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401246 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401253 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401260 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198403 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401308 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401315 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401411 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401418 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403439 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403453 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403460 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401640 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401647 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186221418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402160 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186221418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402167 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186221441 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402106 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186221457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402269 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186221966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220402 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186221966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402113 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186221966 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186221966 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-y(t)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186221966 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) + y(t) + 1; y^{′}(t) = 2x(t) - y(t) - 1$\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186221966 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0 \\\\ 2x-y-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3y-3=0\\,⇒\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(-1)+1=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186229133 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402132 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186229143 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400463 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186229143 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400470 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186229143 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404050 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186229148 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402119 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186229194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402388 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186229675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228214 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186229675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402126 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186229675 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186229675 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186229675 1071 "${x^{′}(t) =-4y(t); y^{′}(t) = 4x(t)$\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: $e^{At} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (4t) & -\\sin (4t) \\\\ \\sin (4t) & \\cos (4t)\\end{bmatrix}$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.*" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186229675 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399357 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1069 "q-101736" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1071 "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u$\nII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z$\nIII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1072 #uuid "79e7ca1d-1ed8-5888-8523-0ec8ed90db1c" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1074 "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2(u-1)^{2}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2(a-1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2(2-1)^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2(a-1)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{z}^{3}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integral II is divergent." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399358 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399359 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399360 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399361 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399362 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399362 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1080 #uuid "027a26b9-e238-53ff-b245-735c0d2abc04" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1081 "II and III only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1084 #uuid "c286d992-e374-59ca-b86a-46b1f01569c8" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399359 1081 "I only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399359 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399359 1084 #uuid "aa7fc32d-93c3-5f24-9422-4d81ca03151b" 13194139533478 17592