gin{aligned}s+3t=-2 \\\\ -4s-10t=5 \\\\ 2s+2t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -10 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=-2,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $s$ and $t$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc=u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $u$ does *not* lie on the plane $Π.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399468 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399469 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399468 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399470 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399468 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399471 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399468 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399472 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399468 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399473 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399468 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399468 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399468 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399473 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399468 1080 #uuid "3ded2025-f849-5e24-ae50-154d00e66f84" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399469 1081 "12" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399469 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399469 1084 #uuid "5469c2f4-10d4-551a-8387-1f41e195f9f3" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399470 1081 "-9" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399470 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399470 1084 #uuid "b5aaf75e-0319-5e2f-b6a1-406f9f457622" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399471 1081 "-18" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399471 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399471 1084 #uuid "6f0f386a-54e4-5bbd-999d-c5b1e894fea2" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399472 1081 "6" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399472 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399472 1084 #uuid "ea345608-0f4c-5f8a-9cea-a53c46ba68a9" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399473 1081 "$u$ does not lie on $Π$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399473 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399473 1084 #uuid "f370dd07-5630-5589-a9df-b8eef801b693" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399475 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399474 1069 "q-105281" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399474 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399474 1071 "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399474 1072 #uuid "7f004a40-028a-581c-ab25-3ac8bd7f4fe7" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399474 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={x}^{2}-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=8{y}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y=0 \\\\ 8{y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{({x}^{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 8{x}^{4}-x & =0 \\\\ x(8{x}^{3}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y={x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,y={0}^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{y}^{2}-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)=-1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{y}^{2}-x)=16y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 16y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))={0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}=-1<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}))=1\\cdot 4-(-1)^{2}=3>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})={h}_{11}=1>0.$ Therefore, we have a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399476 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399477 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399478 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399479 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399480 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399476 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1080 #uuid "905988c5-8a51-5ced-aa99-8546923bfeea" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399476 1081 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399476 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399476 1084 #uuid "984bdcd8-65fe-53ce-9365-592fb416acc7" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399477 1081 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399477 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399477 1084 #uuid "6f09a46f-378b-5666-b31d-7d594024f410" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399478 1081 "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399478 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399478 1084 #uuid "6299c235-6bfa-5841-89fe-0705634d245b" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399479 1081 "$f(x,y)$ has local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(1,\\frac{1}{2})$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399479 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399479 1084 #uuid "2d302c69-b32c-57f8-ae7a-4cd8d92c0343" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399480 1081 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(1,\\frac{1}{2})$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399480 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399480 1084 #uuid "d62b096f-ff9c-5138-8170-e9ee40a546da" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399482 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399481 1069 "q-105338" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399481 1071 "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399481 1072 #uuid "e1b5f6e8-a9c9-5be3-86f7-77f082bfcf04" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399481 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x}=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2(0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x})=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{1}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=det({f}^{″}(1,0))=0\\cdot (-2)-{1}^{2}=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399483 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399484 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399484 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1080 #uuid "7119470b-d6b1-5d18-94e0-b83f9d1bc35d" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399483 1081 "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(e,0)$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399483 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399483 1084 #uuid "3afd00a5-3aad-54a4-9a70-7c2101ecdf05" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399484 1081 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399484 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399484 1084 #uuid "31d284