e in the parametric form $r=p+tv+sw$, we have that its normal vector is $n=v\\times w.$ In our case, the normal vector to $Π$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =⟨4,3,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,5⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =15i-20j+8k \\\\  & =⟨15,-20,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane $Π$ in dot product form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ & =⟨10,-5,12⟩\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ & =346.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨4,-2,3⟩+w⟨5,-1,2⟩$ into the equation of the plane in dot product form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ & =346 \\\\ ⟨4+5w,-2-w,3+2w⟩\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ & =346 \\\\ 15(4+5w)-20(-2-w)+8(3+2w) & =346 \\\\ 60+75w+40+20w+24+16w & =346 \\\\ 111w & =222 \\\\ w & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $q$ of the intersection point $Q,$ we substitute $w=2$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q & =⟨4+5(2),-2-(2),3+2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨14,-4,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $Q(14,-4,7).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399644 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399645 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399646 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399647 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399648 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399643 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399643 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399643 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399648 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399643 1080 #uuid "1e560