045585 true] [17592186399663 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399664 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399663 1069 "q-109158" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399663 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399663 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399663 1072 #uuid "8cbfb9b8-f301-5858-97fc-1b90fa72d9ae" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399663 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{{y}^{-1}}{x}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}\\,.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399664 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399665 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399664 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399666 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399664 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399667 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399664 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399668 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399664 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399669 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399664 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399664 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399664 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399666 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399664 1080 #uuid "782ea541-65bb-5fe7-a7fe-a730b04302d9" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399665 1081 "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{C-x}" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399665 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399665 1084 #uuid "6773b545-4c85-529f-8c68-3b883278b649" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399666 1081 "y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}