ponding to $\\lambda =-1,$ and\n\n- one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(2) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A+I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A+I)y=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]=z,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A+I)^{2}y=(A+I)z=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399713 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399714 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399713 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399715 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399713 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399716 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399713 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399717 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399713 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399718 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399713 1076 #edb/re