2}}{4-{y}^{2}}$\nIII. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-4}$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399904 1072 #uuid "53ab2db1-0308-5084-926c-c3a100e39237" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399904 1074 "![](images/5f/5f87023b47e66436ca4f6caa8e8cc651e7de80ab09e346d955e1bb12eea211b6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 2.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )$ and positive for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4-{y}^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to \\pm 2.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )$ and  positive for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{4}{{y}^{2}}-1)}\\to -1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-4}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y\\in (-2,2)$ and positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399905 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399906 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399905 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399907 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399905 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399908 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399905 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399909 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399905 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399910 131941395