b0d73cd2db3912b37e172f714131.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ intersects the line $x-3y=-4$ (sketched above)." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399953 1072 #uuid "f9516424-4bd5-5885-82ea-4588d28a4cdd" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399953 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation $x-3y=-4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3})}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{8x}{9}+\\frac{16}{9} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{10x}{9}+\\frac{25}{9} & =\\frac{45}{9} \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{10x}{9}-\\frac{20}{9} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-1,2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-1,1).$ Next, we plug in $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the line $x-3y=-4$ intersects the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ at the points $(2,2)$ and $(-1,1).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399954 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399955 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399954 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399956 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399954 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399957 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399954 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399958 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399954 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399