5 true] [17592186400232 1074 "![](images/85/857a4b0109ddc20535b65ffe16191494ab9fdef01b6def354fcd68b862b98679.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-cos⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $1.5$ to give $y=-1.5cos⁡x$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-1.5cos⁡x.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400233 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400234 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400233 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400235 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400233 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400236 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400233 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400237 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400233 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400238 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400233 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400233 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400233 1079 #edb/ref 17592186400238 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400233 1080 #uuid "56e9c54d-acd0-56fe-8076-95dec659fb84" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400234 1081 "y=1.5cos⁡(x)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400234 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400234 1084 #uuid "c4e173d4-f5b8-5ccc-9008-d91b45244f8c" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400235 1081 "y=cos⁡(x)-1.5" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400235 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400235 1084 #uuid "4c24a715-ead1-58d4-8292-0189666d823a" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400236 1081 "y=cos⁡(x)+1.5" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400236 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400236 1084 #uuid "c1cd17dd-96e3-5b25-bf97-b5e326ff0ab9" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400237 1081 "y=-cos⁡(x-1.5)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400237 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400237 1084 #uuid "774b4b4b-55ca-5041-80c5-d41b0058a5c0" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400238 1081 "y=-1.5cos⁡(x)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400238 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400238 1084 #uuid "c37c2d1e-7ba4-57eb-9816-faa0ae5b47ae" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186400240 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400239 1069 "q-126774" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400239 1071 "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}}=\\frac{3x}{{1}^{3/2}}-\\frac{{3}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{{3}^{3}{x}^{3}}{{3}^{3/2}}-\\frac{{3}^{4}{x}^{4}}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400239 1072 #uuid "e567d72e-c85f-58b7-9ff5-c8e3c41c5382" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400239 1074 "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+2}{3}^{n+1}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3/2}}{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-3x{n}^{3/2}}{(n+1)^{3/2}}| \\\\  & =3|-x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{3/2} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $|x|<\\frac{1}{3}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{3},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{3},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{(\\frac{3}{3})}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.   So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(-1)^{n}{(\\frac{3}{3})}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n+1}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-(1+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯)\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the $p$-series test.   So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}].$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400240 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400241 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400240 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400242 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400240 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400243 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400240 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400244 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400240 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400245 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400240 1076 #edb/ref 1