{e}^{4{x}^{2}}+(8x)({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){e}^{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =[2x+8{x}^{3}-10x]{e}^{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(8{x}^{3}-8x){e}^{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{x}^{3}-8x){e}^{4{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{4{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{4{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-8x & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 8x(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,-1,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400590 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400591 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400590 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400592 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400590 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400593 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400590 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400594 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400590 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400595 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400590 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400590 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400590 1079 #edb/ref 17592186400592 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400590 1080 #uuid "80b83c99-19f6-547f-98fa-adc91ffefc5f" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400591 1081 "$x=-1$ and $x=3$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400591 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400591 1084 #uuid "d89fcce3-b832-5e4c-bf7c-02c1c17153f9" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186400592 1081 "