eta ⟩" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401095 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401095 1084 #uuid "94ddc772-4e3c-53f3-95c3-4e3a3d691c6d" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401097 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401096 1069 "q-157052" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401096 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401096 1071 "$$\n\\boxed{}\\,x=\\boxed{}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401096 1072 #uuid "f81ecce3-b49e-5261-aee2-21f0b16a1f87" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401096 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ R\\,\\,x & ={Q}^{T}\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are $R$ and ${Q}^{T}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401097 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401098 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401097 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401099 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401097 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401100 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401097 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401101 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401097 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401102 1319413