of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+4)}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{10}{3})}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+10 & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-4,2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-4,-2).$ Next, we plug in $x=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the line $x-3y=2$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=20$ at the points $(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401111 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401112 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401111 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401113 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401111 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401114 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401111 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401115 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401111 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401116 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401111 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401111 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401111 1079 #edb/ref 17592186401113 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401111 1080 #uuid "0c104c61-200a-5174-998d-683d5dd55c83" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401112 1081 "$(-4,-2)$ an