that $5=(p+q\\text{i})(3+\\text{i})$, calculate $p$ and $q.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401171 1072 #uuid "050685d3-106a-556e-94cf-f2ab6b33d435" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401171 1074 "First, we divide both sides of the equation by $3+\\text{i}$ to isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =(p+q\\text{i})(3+\\text{i}) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3+\\text{i}} & =p+q\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to simplify the fraction on the left-hand side, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(5)(3-\\text{i})}{(3+\\text{i})(3-\\text{i})} & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{15-5\\text{i}}{{3}^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2}} & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{15-5\\text{i}}{9+1} & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{15-5\\text{i}}{10} & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i} & =p+q\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get $\\frac{3}{2}=p.$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get $-\\frac{1}{2}=q.$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $q=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401172 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401173 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401172 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401174 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401172 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401175 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401172 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401176 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401172 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401177 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401172 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401172 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401172 1079 #edb/ref 17592186401177 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401172 1080 #uuid "56ae83c2-5ded-53be-a27e-61769cd01afe" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401173 1081 "p=-\\frac{3}{2},q=\\frac{3}{5}" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [