2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(x)}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4de9d9df51efcec67253a1c7118466f29459ffd5c9136c144d33500ec5557bc.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{2y}-1\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we split $D$ into two type II regions.\n\n![](images/18/18bb3e55f076e022176bb60df27d5665ed2d91d75d618969c04ce192378db922.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the lower region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(z+1)^{2}.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the upper region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=4-2z.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}(z+1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-2z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{(z+1)^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-2z}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401336 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401337 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401336 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401338 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401336 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401339 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401336 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401340 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401336 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401341 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401336 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139