-6998-5bbb-863e-83fa6322632b" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401873 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401872 1069 "q-21353" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401872 1071 "![](images/4a/4aa2bca8faab077c47fb968882579233babe7d323ba3120088e8ea5599fbd570.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x={e}^{t},\\,y=t$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=2$ (shown above)?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401872 1072 #uuid "a60afb71-a1f2-5589-86e8-84aaf38f221a" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401872 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{({e}^{t})^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401873 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401874 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401873 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401875 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401873 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401876 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401873 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401877 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401873 1075 #edb/ref 17