186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401978 1084 #uuid "ea3248ac-1443-5057-98d3-f07c503f35c9" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401979 1081 "$x=-4,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401979 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401979 1084 #uuid "6e0e922d-f9cb-5465-99c6-9d8aecf68d80" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401981 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401980 1069 "q-31219" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401980 1071 "![](images/5d/5d776a61d1039639d91e2c667c68965d3e2c901cdc926e8c54e6356cf1d87c2e.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401980 1072 #uuid "98bca65a-5215-5c3c-9419-26f83fa4001a" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401980 1074 "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$ \n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-6$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-6$ and $x=2.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401982 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401983 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401984 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401985 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401986 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401981 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401981 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401981 1079 #edb/ref 17592186401982 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401981 1080 #uuid "2def65a7-d460-55e9-9537-f22df65257ea" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401982 1081 "$x=-6$ and $x=2$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401982 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401982 1084 #uuid "7f18ae8c-d034-5c1c-af41-b32f52e9728d" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401983 1081 "$x=-6$ and $x=-2$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401983 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401983 1084 #uuid "3c2ac919-d0f0-55be-b66b-077be2492a74" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401984 1081 "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401984 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401984 1084 #uuid "b6faa5c0-12ea-54ed-86d4-4c1d75cbd567" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401985 1081 "x=-2" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401985 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401985 1084 #uuid "96d32a85-ec32-5353-9868-3cb1ac090a40" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401986 1081 "$x=-6,\\,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=2$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401986 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401986 1084 #uuid "a41d1397-7de6-584c-b009-e1eca0c2916b" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401988 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401987 1069 "q-31872" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401987 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401987 1071 "The inverse of the matrix $J$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[J\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401987 1072 #uuid "0ac0783b-5b73-5c8c-959b-6029e5d56c68" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401987 1074 "We write down $J$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $J$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[J\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${J}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{J}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186401988 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401989