6045585 true] [17592186402379 1084 #uuid "79867f36-2c63-53a2-b0d7-906e247536ec" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402380 1081 "I" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402380 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402380 1084 #uuid "7b1cfc22-b026-5ac0-aed5-a3a643bfff05" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402382 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402381 1069 "q-345274" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402381 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402381 1071 "$$\n-7{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+(x-5)y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402381 1072 #uuid "e617971d-af12-59ac-a1e1-3a955cbb7317" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402381 1074 "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series so that they use the same index of summation.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =-7{x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }7((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 2x{y}^{′} & =2x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (x-5)y & =(x-5){\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)y={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)y={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-5{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =-7({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 2x{y}^{′} & =2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-5{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (-7{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +2\\lambda -5){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (-7{\\lambda }^{2}+9\\lambda -5){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -5){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (-7{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -5){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -5){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 7{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402382 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402383 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402382 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402384 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402382 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402385 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402382 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402386 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402382 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402387 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402382 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402382 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402382 1079 #edb/ref 17592186402383 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402382 1080 #uuid "f7e55e1f-40ba-53f0-bfe4-bffbf8e87e07" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402383 1081 "7{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +5=0" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402383 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402383 1084 #uuid "7b1137f1-8456-5fcb-b627-0a6bfcd1ccb9" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402384 1081 "7{\\lambda }^{