ing Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402530 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402531 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402530 1075 #edb/r