gned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402648 1072 #uuid "5efb10ac-d6d7-5644-9104-60d88e699342" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402648 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 1\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=1$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1+(-2)\\text{i}=1-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}0+1\\text{i} \\\\ 2+0\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =1∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402649 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402650 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402649 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402651 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402649 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402652 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402649 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402653 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402649 1075 #edb/ref 175921864