f81e-5caa-b027-397d46c4a3e3" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402741 1081 "{\\int }_{1+{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402741 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402741 1084 #uuid "86a6fd7d-2914-5ff6-a175-96b59582d072" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402742 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402743 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402742 1069 "q-41741" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402742 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402742 1071 "A small business collected data on the number of hours of telephone usage and the total amount (in hundreds of dollars) of the company's weekly operating expenses. This data is shown below.   \n\n| Hours $(x)$ | **$2$** | **$2.4$** | **$2.8$** | **$3.2$** | **$3.6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Expenses $(y)$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the company's weekly operating expenses if the telephone is used for a total of $4$ hours per week, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}40.8 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 88\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402742 1072 #uuid "d0007be5-4b39-5b13-9d0c-f56e380ce0f1" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402742 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2.4 \\\\ 1 & 2.8 \\\\ 1 & 3.2 \\\\ 1 & 3.6\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}40.8 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 88\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-1+2.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the amount of the company's weekly operating expenses if the telephone is used for a total of $4$ hours, we substitute $x=4$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1+2.5(4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the company's weekly operating expenses will be $$900.$ \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=4$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402743 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402744 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402743 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402745 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402743 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402746 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402743 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402747 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402743 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402748 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402743 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186402743 