7592186404022 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404022 1071 "![](images/b3/b3bb8c00ab2e0ef3e7c1bc21726458e718e67897f28093321884bd1c67ca768f.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404022 1072 #uuid "dc9954e3-432a-5a64-87f7-a9d25a9f95d6" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404022 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-4,-1)$ and passes through the point $(5,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-4,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+1)^{2}=4p(x+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(5,5),$ we substitute $x=5$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5+1)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 36 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4(0+4) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ y & =-5,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $-5$ and $3.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404023 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404024 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404023 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404025 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404023 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404026 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404023 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404027 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404023 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404028 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404023 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404023 1077 "selection" 131941395