45585 true] [17592186404029 1072 #uuid "e640b0e6-4f4b-5358-84ca-251a991cee48" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404029 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*In a controlled environment, the **temperature** $T$ of a hot drink **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **cube of the temperature** $T.$*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *temperature* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *cube of the temperature.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{T}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the temperature *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{3}}.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404030 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404031 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404030 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404032 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404030 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404033 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404030 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404034 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404030 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404035 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404030 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404030 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404030 1079 #edb/ref 17592186404033 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404030 1080 #uuid "54fb041f-00b7-53a7-b2c7-5721abae068d" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404031 1081 "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k{T}^{3},\\,\\,k>0" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404031 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404031 1084 #uuid "967d9b2c-1fda-53bc-ae0d-e10db710fdf5" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404032 1081 "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt[3]{T},\\,\\,k>0" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404032 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404032 1084 #uuid "b9ea423a-8ac0-584d-a9cf-f4c2ccea48eb" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404033 1081 "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{3}},\\,\\,k>0" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404033 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404033 1084 #uuid "021d55c8-03a2-563f-ad3a-db919a1e2246" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404034 1081 "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k{T}^{3},\\,\\,k>0" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404034 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404034 1084 #uuid "e4736ffb-9caa-5548-a49e-35adb9ed2dfb" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404035 1081 "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{\\sqrt[3]{T}},\\,\\,k>0" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404035 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404035 1084 #uuid "313b345a-3675-5490-b55d-49f946a9d2d1" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404036 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404037 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404036 1069 "q-79438" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404036 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404036 1071 "Given that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$ for $s>0.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404036 1072 #uuid "f9e7904b-a424-5faa-b622-0f16d31ff921" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404036 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}+L{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}+3L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}+3L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}+3\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{3s}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3s+2}{{s}^{2}+4},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404037 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404038 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404037 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404039 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404037 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404040 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404037 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404041 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404037 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404042 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404037 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404037 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404037 1079 #edb/ref 17592186404042 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404037 1080 #uuid "fd0d8007-7775-5b7a-a407-ec01a5ac1b20" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404038 1081 "\\frac{3s-1}{{s}^{2}+4}" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404038 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404038 1084 #uuid "72faae25-5e2c-5641-a31e-198f13b85833" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404039 1081 "\\frac{s+2}{{s}^{2}+2}" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404039 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404039 1084 #uuid "60fd44b5-40fc-5e2e-ba3b-d25072a0023b" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404040 1081 "\\frac{{s}^{2}-3}{{s}^{2}+4}" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404040 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404040 1084 #uuid "d44ae010-dbbe-5f4d-b0e4-19ecdfbf0caa" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404041 1081 "\\frac{{s}^{2}-1}{{s}^{2}+2}" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404041 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404041 1084 #uuid "d9d0ddbf-ea24-5c90-92e4-c9ae79a6aded" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404042 1081 "\\frac{3s+2}{{s}^{2}+4}" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404042 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404042 1084 #uuid "c8338925-abcb-5816-8940-b12198bb3d05" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404043 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404044 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404043 1069 "q-79577" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404043 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404043 1071 "Is the function $f(x,y,z)=2xyz-{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}z$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404043 1072 #uuid "26800737-1d20-5fc8-93e8-d8bcbcdef142" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404043 1074 "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{n}f(x,y,z).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty,tz) & =2(tx)(ty)(tz)-(ty)^{3}+(ty)^{2}(tz) \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}xyz-{t}^{3}{y}^{3}+{t}^{3}{y}^{2}z \\\\  & ={t}^{3}(2xyz-{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}z) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}f(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{3}f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y,z)$ is $3.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404044 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404045 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404044 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404046 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404044 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404047 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404044 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404048 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404044 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404049 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404044 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404044 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404044 1079 #edb/ref 17592186404047 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404044 1080 #uuid "040589f2-2b68-5371-a3aa-d45a057e519d" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404045 1081 "Homogeneous of degree $2$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404045 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404045 1084 #uuid "7c29ea0e-f357-5dc3-99ca-2bae8aec29b6" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404046 1081 "Homogeneous of degree $1$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404046 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404046 1084 #uuid "a5ea6ef9-6b75-5257-b121-1b523e9b915b" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404047 1081 "Homogeneous of degree $3$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404047 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404047 1084 #uuid "666c638e-5bf2-5c76-ab61-9718d548bf8d" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404048 1081 "Homogeneous of degree $4$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404048 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404048 1084 #uuid "8a7dd162-1ea2-5c31-abfa-4c05e5a985ad" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404049 1081 "Not homogeneous" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404049 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404049 1084 #uuid "ac02fad1-6d75-529b-b2da-c5e1cd09aae4" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404051 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404050 1069 "q-79609" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404050 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404050 1071 "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nI. ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+y$\nII. ${D}_{2}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-xy$\nIII. ${D}_{3}(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404050 1072 #uuid "25cb2680-651e-5a50-81aa-a03a420f17ff" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404050 1074 "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{1}]+b[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+bx\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-ax{y}_{1}-bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x{y}_{1}]+b[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is **not** linear.\nTo see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\na{D}_{3}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{3}({y}_{2})=a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.\\,\\times \n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404051 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404052 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404051 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404053 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404051 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404054 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404051 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404055 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404051 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404056 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404051 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 1759218604