194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404057 1072 #uuid "144b4980-16bd-56a0-bd2d-6e2904dde295" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404057 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1/2}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{2}\\,.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{dz}{dx}=\\frac{1}{2{y}^{1/2}}\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}=2z\\frac{dz}{dx}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =8xz-\\frac{4}{x}{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =4x-\\frac{2}{x}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =4x-\\frac{2}{x}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z & =4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=4x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (4x) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z) & =4{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{2}z & ={x}^{4}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+C}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{1/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+C}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{({x}^{4}+C)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404058 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404059 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404058 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404060 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404058 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404061 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404058 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404062 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404058 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404063 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404058 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404058 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404058 1079 #edb/ref 17592186404059 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404058 1080 #uuid "8853e332-7fcd-583c-9b4c-e2dadf8be7ca" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404059 1081 "y=\\frac{({x}^{4}+C)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404059 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404059 1084 #uuid "2ee4f807-4aa5-5a16-8059-65d96394945a" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404060 1081 "y=\\frac{{x}^{8}+C}{{x}^{2}}" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404060 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404060 1084 #uuid "223caf6f-af28-5ef9-9a4f-1a520db495e0" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404061 1081 "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}+C}{{x}^{2}}" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404061 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404061 1084 #uuid "337814ed-5fcc-59cf-8f04-ff8ec9f234ec" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404062 1081 "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}+C}{{x}^{2}}" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404062 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404062 1084 #uuid "7a73c3ff-fb2c-5b83-a30f-1c7130a2b411" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404063 1081 "y=\\frac{{x}^{8}+C}{{x}^{4}}" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404063 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404063 1084 #uuid "41ee67ee-bf37-531f-ae68-93bf2d23f87f" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404064 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404065 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404064 1069 "q-79799" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404064 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404064 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{4t}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nI. $y(t)={e}^{-t-1}$\nII. $y(t)=7{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-4t}$\nIII. $y(t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{4}$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404064 1072 #uuid "742f0d7e-0be6-5200-afaf-b211266d03e0" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404064 1074 "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{4t}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(t)={e}^{-t-1}=\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{e}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{e},{c}_{2}=0.$\n\n- The function $y(t)=7{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-4t}$ is **not** a solution of the given equation because ${e}^{-4t}$ is not a solution.\n\n- The function $y(t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{4}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=0,{c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404065 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404066 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404065 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404067 13194139