\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404126 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404126 1084 #uuid "3093f458-0d1e-55d2-af6e-2473cd7616a9" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404128 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404127 1069 "q-80766" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404127 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=y(1+x){e}^{x}+ax{y}^{3}+3{x}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=bx{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404127 1072 #uuid "f63374b4-82d5-5092-bcef-8ec87e149ab1" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404127 1074 "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y(1+x){e}^{x}+ax{y}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(bx{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =b(1+x){e}^{x}-6x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2}=b(1+x){e}^{x}-6x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $(1+x){e}^{x}$ and $x{y}^{2},$ respectively, we see that $b=1$ and $a=-2.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404128 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404129 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404128 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404130 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404128 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404131 131941395