045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404335 1071 "The equation $-4{w}^{2}-4w+11=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404335 1072 #uuid "7895edb1-f2c2-5302-ad9e-dc0b6ea9c033" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404335 1074 "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}-4w+11) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}+4w-11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}+4w-11 & =0 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}+w) & =11 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}w+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =11 \\\\ 4[{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} & =11 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-1 & =11 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =11+1 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=12.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{12}{4} \\\\ {(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ |w+\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ w+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ w & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404336 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404337 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404336 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404338 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404336 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404339 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404336 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404340 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404336 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404341 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404336 1076 #edb/ref 175