5d76008af454d5.png)\n\nSince the point $(0,-3)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,-4),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1),\\,(2,-4).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,-3) & =2(0)(-3)+6(0)-3 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ f(-2,-4) & =2(-2)(-4)+6(-2)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-1,-1) & =2(-1)(-1)+6(-1)-3 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(1,-1) & =2(1)(-1)+6(1)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(2,-4) & =2(2)(-4)+6(2)-3 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the points $(-1,-1)$ and $(2,-4),$\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(-2,-4)$ and $(1,-1).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404358 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404359 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404360 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404361 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404362 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404357 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404357 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404357 1079 #edb/ref 17592186404360 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404357 1080 #uuid "c1c6b9bc-2cad-5aef-8fbc-0df5f811dfbb" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404358 1081 "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404358 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true