alues, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}6-2y,\\,-2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6-2y=0 \\\\ -2x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,3).$ Since the point $(0,3)$ does not belong to the closed region of $D,$ we do not include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =6(t)-2(t)(0) \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =6(t)-2(t)(2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3}+2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation has no solution inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$ So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain two candidates:\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-12$ | $12$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-12$ at the point $(-2,0)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $12$ at the point $(2,0).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404364 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404365 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404364 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404366 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404364 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404367 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404364 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404368 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404364 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404369 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404364 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404364 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404364 1079 #edb/ref 17592186404367 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404364 1080 #uuid "cd6de7a6-023f-5e00-9b0c-d361277d5ae6" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404365 1081 "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404365 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404365 1084 #uuid "5c6ddfc8-d3e9-5bef-b036-cd7895639820" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404366 1081 "${f}_{min}=-6$ and ${f}_{max}=6$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404366 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404366 1084 #uuid "c9416bc5-9684-5819-b799-c1e0f49a858d" 13194139533478 17592186045585 tru