186404772 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404772 1071 "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=-3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404772 1072 #uuid "0d232faf-2de0-5a39-91fc-eea4fa2a2bb3" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404772 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2,\\,-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404773 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404774 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404773 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404775 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404773 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404776 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404773 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404777 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404773 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404778 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404773 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404773 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404773 1079 #edb/ref 17592186404775 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404773 1080 #uuid "af751c8a-d34c-5ece-8db8-ee7406d5299f" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404774 1081 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404774 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404774 1084 #uuid "22954aba-c66f-5f85-9e45-9aa6ce4d72bb" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404775 1081 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404775 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13