riables above reduces $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $6{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404807 1072 #uuid "ac56a346-b90f-50d3-b0af-5c5bfbbf564d" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404807 1074 "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{5{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}-4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=8\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}-4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2} & =8 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}-4\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2})+4\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =8 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+8{y}_{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+8{y}_{2} & =8 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2}) & =8 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2}+1-1) & =8 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+4{({y}_{2}+1)}^{2}-4 & =8 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+4{({y}_{2}+1)}^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{z}_{1}^{2}+4{z}_{2}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{