{\\lambda }_{1}=1.$ Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{1}$ corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{1})=1.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{-1}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-(-1)I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). This implies that we will have $3-2=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-1})=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is *not* diagonalizable, since \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{1})+\\text{dim}({V}_{-1})=1+1=2\\ne 3.\\,\\times \n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404926 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404927 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404926 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404928 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404926 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404929 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404926 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404930 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404926 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404931 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404926 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404926 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404926 1079 #edb/ref 17592186404931 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404926 1080 #uuid "de7ae