ch that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & ={(12x-{x}^{3})}^{′}{e}^{12x-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =(12-3{x}^{2}){e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12-3{x}^{2}){e}^{12x-{x}^{3}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince ${e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(4-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (2-x)(2+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404982 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404983 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404984 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404985 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404986 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404981 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404981 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404981 1079 #edb/ref 17592186404985 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404981 1080 #uuid "0a891f36-9832-5e16-a500-7251af8a1737" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404982 1081 "$x=2$ only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186404982 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true]