}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=2.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405002 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405003 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405002 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405004 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405002 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405005 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405002 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405006 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405002 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405007 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405002 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405002 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405002 1079 #edb/ref 17592186405004 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405002 1080 #uuid "dcf1d3ea-9362-58c4-b795-b8796b5019b7" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405003 1081 "$x=2$ only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405003 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405003 1084 #uuid "065b8702-9c1f-5408-a110-0b6b1f931f94" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405004 1081 "$x=1$ and $x=2$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405004 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405004 1084 #uuid "7e04b74f-d9c6-59e7-a8ee-004d2123563f" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405005 1081 "$x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=2$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405005 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405005 1084 #uuid "625ac297-0116-50dc-b32e-593a5c25fd5b" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405006 1081 "$x=-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405006 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405006 1084 #uuid "2f10324a-c1cb-5f86-99bd-f736b0ed8050" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405007 1081 "$x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405007 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405007 1084 #uuid "acea3269-c2cc-5d39-87be-8ac3b32540bc" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405009 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405008 1069 "q-9926" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405008 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405008 1071 "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}}=\\frac{x}{{1}^{3/2}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{3}^{3/2}}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405008 1072 #uuid "fbe3ba98-4d51-53d7-a612-90e7db738fe7" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405008 1074 "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3/2}}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{3/2}|x| \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{3/2} \\\\  & =|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $|x|<1.$ So, the radius of convergence is $1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-1,1).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-1$ and $x=1$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{1}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the $p$-series test.  So the endpoint $x=1$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{{1}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=-1$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-1,1].$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405009 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405010 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405009 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405011 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405009 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405012 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405009 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405013 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405009 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405014 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405009 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405009 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405009 1079 #edb/ref 17592186405011 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405009 1080 #uuid "dc050dae-f829-533e-a886-22db5daf03b6" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405010 1081 "x\\in [-\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\,,\\,\\frac{2}{3}]" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405010 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405010 1084 #uuid "8f06d73f-7ac7-568b-9656-314a94895833" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186405011 1081 "x\